<article class="env-lemma" id="0G69">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0G69">Lemma <span data-tag="0G69">33.8.12</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $X$ be a scheme over $k$. If $X$ is irreducible and has a dense set of $k$-rational points, then $X$ is geometrically irreducible. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $k'/k$ be a finite extension of fields and let $Z, Z' \subset X_{k'}$ be irreducible components. It suffices to show $Z = Z'$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/038I" data-tag="038I">33.8.9</a>. By Lemma <a href="/tag/04KX" data-tag="04KX">33.8.11</a> we have $p(Z) = p(Z') = X$ where $p : X_{k'} \to X$ is the projection. If $Z \not= Z'$ then $Z \cap Z'$ is nowhere dense in $X_{k'}$ and hence $p(Z \cap Z')$ is not dense by Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/03HX" data-tag="03HX">29.49.7</a>; here we also use that $p$ is a finite morphism as the base change of the finite morphism $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(k') \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(k)$, see Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/01WL" data-tag="01WL">29.45.6</a>. Thus we can pick a $k$-rational point $x \in X$ with $x \not\in p(Z \cap Z')$. Since the residue field of $x$ is $k$ we see that $p^{-1}(\{ x\} ) = \{ x'\} $ where $x' \in X_{k'}$ is a point whose residue field is $k'$. Since $x \in p(Z) = p(Z')$ we conclude that $x' \in Z \cap Z'$ which is the contradiction we were looking for. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>